ibuting random success to their own skill and, as a consequence, increasing risk taking. Although past performance does not predict future success for these traders, traders increase trade sizes, trade size variability, and number of trades with gains, and less with losses. There is a large discontinuity in all of these trading variables around zero past week returns: e.g., traders increase their trade size dramatically following winning weeks, relative to losing weeks. The effects are stronger for novice traders, consistent with more intense \u201clearning\u201d in early trading periods.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.2540584","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2014,12,24]],"date-time":"2014-12-24T11:01:05Z","timestamp":1419418865000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":2,"title":["Uninformative Feedback and Risk Taking: Evidence from Retail Forex Trading"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9743-7441","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Itzhak","family":"Ben-David","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Justin","family":"Birru","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Viktor","family":"Prokopenya","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","reference":[{"issue":"1","key":"ref1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"133","DOI":"10.1016\/0749-5978(87)90049-5","article-title":"Two Methods of Reducing Overconfidence","volume":"39","author":"Hal R Arkes","year":"1987","journal-title":"Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes"},{"issue":"2","key":"ref2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"773","DOI":"10.1111\/0022-1082.00226","article-title":"Trading Is Hazardous to Your Wealth: The Common Stock Investment Performance of Individual Investors","volume":"55","author":"Brad M Barber","year":"2000","journal